      "text": "To determine the minimum possible value of the sum \\(\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a}\\), we start by applying the method of Lagrange multipliers or by using the AM-GM (Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean) Inequality. However, here we use a more straightforward approach which involves inequalities.\n\nFirst, assume \\(a = 2b = 4c = k\\), where \\(k\\) is a common positive real number. Then, we can rewrite the expression in terms of \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{2b} = \\frac{k}{2k} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{4c} = \\frac{k/2}{k/2} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{c}{8a} = \\frac{k/4}{2k} = \\frac{1}{8}\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a} = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{4}{8} + \\frac{4}{8} + \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{9}{8}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to prove that \\(\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a} \\geq \\frac{9}{8}\\) holds for any positive real numbers \\(a, b, c\\). By the AM-GM inequality, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a} \\geq 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{a}{2b} \\cdot \\frac{b}{4c} \\cdot \\frac{c}{8a}}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the expression inside the cube root:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{2b} \\cdot \\frac{b}{4c} \\cdot \\frac{c}{8a} = \\frac{1}{128}\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a} \\geq 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{128}} = 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nHowever, we need to verify that this lower bound is achievable. We can set \\(a = 2b = 4c = k\\) to check if \\(\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a} = \\frac{9}{8}\\). From our earlier substitution, we can see that this indeed holds true.\n\nTherefore, the minimum possible value of the sum \\(\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a}\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{9}{8}}\\).",
